[[:db/retract 17592186204255 :question/answer-fields 17592186200241] [:db/retract 17592186204255 :question/answer-fields 17592186200244] [:db/retract 17592186204255 :question/answer-fields 17592186200247] [:db/retract 17592186204255 :question/answer-fields 17592186200250] [:db/retract 17592186204255 :question/answer-fields 17592186200235] [:db/retract 17592186204255 :question/answer-fields 17592186200238] [:db/retract 17592186204267 :question/answer-fields 17592186200369] [:db/retract 17592186204267 :question/answer-fields 17592186200366] [:db/retract 17592186204259 :question/answer-fields 17592186200280] [:db/retract 17592186204259 :question/answer-fields 17592186200288] [:db/retract 17592186204259 :question/answer-fields 17592186200284] [:db/retract 17592186204260 :question/answer-fields 17592186200296] [:db/retract 17592186204260 :question/answer-fields 17592186200304] [:db/retract 17592186204260 :question/answer-fields 17592186200292] [:db/retract 17592186204260 :question/answer-fields 17592186200300] [:db/retract 17592186204256 :question/answer-fields 17592186200253] {:db/id 17592186203924 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3a2bda0c-9e42-53f1-a525-c0492f5a91dc" "question-83135e57-d72a-5f9f-8787-4bc48f405724" "question-b24ff238-78b5-5737-b5c9-c38496b2d157"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203699 :question/problem "Consider the following relation defined on the set $A = \\{ 2,5,9 \\}.$ \n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (2,2), (2,5), (2,9), (5,9), (9,9) \\big\\}\n$$\n\nDetermine whether the relation is symmetric, antisymmetric, both, or neither." :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y \\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad (y,x) \\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y \\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,x) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x=y.\n$$\n\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x).$\n\nOur relation is defined on $\\{ 2,5,9 \\}.$ Notice that:\n\n- $(2,5) \\in R$ and $(5,2) \\notin R$\n\n- $(2,9) \\in R$ and $(9,2) \\notin R$\n\n- $(5,9) \\in R$ and $(9,5) \\notin R$\n\nThere are no other pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x \\neq y$ in the relation. So, for all $x,y \\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,x) \\in R \\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the relation is antisymmetric." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203925 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d4393a9f-4375-593d-841f-a618d5e585c3" "question-bdbdaa55-4775-5c9f-83c4-cb845bb96951" "question-4d692895-cdfb-5939-b4c3-d559da7a492c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203706 :question/problem "Consider a relation $R$ defined on the set $A = \\{ 1, 4, 9\\}.$ Given that $R$ is symmetric, enter the missing values in the corresponding adjacency matrix below. \n\n|  | $1$ | $4$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\phantom{1}}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\phantom{1}}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\phantom{1}}$ |\n| $4$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\phantom{1}}$ |\n| $9$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y \\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad (y,x) \\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y \\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,x) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x=y.\n$$\n\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x).$\n\nWe are given elements of the adjacency matrix in the following highlighted cells outside the main diagonal:  \n\n|  | $1$ | $4$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\phantom{1}}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\phantom{1}}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\phantom{1}}$ |\n| $4$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\phantom{1}}$ |\n| $9$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nAs a result, the relation contains the pairs $(9,1)$ and $(9,4).$ Since $R$ is be symmetric, we require \n\n$$\n(1,9) \\in R, \\qquad (4,9) \\in  R.\n$$\n\nSo, the cells corresponding to these elements must all equal $1.$\n\n|  | $1$ | $4$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\phantom{1}}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\phantom{1}}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$ |\n| $4$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$ |\n| $9$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nAlso, note the following:\n\n- The symmetric relation condition is equivalent to $(x,y)\\notin R \\Longrightarrow (y,x) \\notin R.$ Therefore, $(4,1)\\notin R$ implies $(1,4) \\notin R.$\n\n- We do not have enough information to determine whether $(1,1)$ is an element of $R.$\n\nLet's add this information to our table.\n\n|  | $1$ | $4$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}?}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$ |\n| $4$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$ |\n| $9$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203926 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-92e553e5-9efc-54d5-9f9a-59a998ba0a7b" "question-84454995-d49e-5edd-80f6-dfbaab2558d5" "question-cd780cfc-a5dd-5c8f-a2c7-e44b9d2a3075" "question-ee6fe66d-09a5-518d-9a32-a533c9b3cc75"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203700 :question/problem "![](images/5c/5c7734813ff7d452ee1a8eccb669ef287eb41e6de3e1abe83a58de98c8316fbd.png)\n\n \n\nWhich of the above digraphs represents a relation, defined on the set $A = \\{p,q,r\\},$ that is neither symmetric nor antisymmetric?" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y \\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad (y,x) \\in R.\n$$\n\nIn terms of a digraph, if we have an arrow $x \\rightarrow y,$ then the arrow $y \\rightarrow x$ must be in the digraph too.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y \\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,x) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x=y.\n$$\n\nSo, only one of the arrows, either $x \\rightarrow y$ or $y \\rightarrow x,$ can be in the digraph of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows loops.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our digraphs.\n\n- Digraph I represents a symmetric relation since for every arrow $x \\rightarrow y$ belonging to the graph, the corresponding inverse arrow $y \\rightarrow x$ also belongs to the graph.\n\n- Digraph II represents an antisymmetric relation since for all possible $x$ and $y$ with $x \\neq y,$ *at most one* of the arrows (either $x \\rightarrow y$ or $y \\rightarrow x,$ but not both) belongs to the digraph.\n\n- Digraph III represents neither a symmetric nor an antisymmetric relation. It's not symmetric since the arrow $p \\rightarrow q$ belongs to the digraph while the arrow $q \\rightarrow p$ does not. It is not antisymmetric since, for example, arrows $p \\rightarrow r$ and $r \\rightarrow p$ belong to the digraph.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203927 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ceafef3e-b20d-5064-9135-0687709767da" "question-16c25cec-ba90-5814-9c8d-34f1bb30c2b1" "question-fe8a5755-a006-512f-a829-ac55db45a0f7" "question-452336ac-2206-5d26-872d-718b80ac9d74" "question-7f3b0fa8-024e-5898-b1f6-7de09c79b487"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203701 :question/problem "Which of the following relations are symmetric? \n\n- $x \\: R \\: y$ if and only if $x \\leq y,$ defined on $\\mathbb{R}$\n\n- $x \\: S \\: y$ if and only if $x \\equiv y \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:6),$ defined on $\\mathbb{Z}$\n\n- $X \\: T \\: Y$ if and only if $X \\cap Y = \\mathbb{N},$ defined on $\\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N})$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y \\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad (y,x) \\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y \\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,x) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x=y.\n$$\n\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations.\n\n- Relation $R$ is not symmetric. Notice that \n\n- $(0,1) \\in R$ since $0 \\leq 1,$ while\n\n- $(1,0) \\notin R$ since $1 \\not \\leq 0.$\n\n- Relation $S$ is symmetric. Indeed, if $(x,y) \\in S$ then $(y,x) \\in S.$ Notice that \n\n$$\nx \\equiv y \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:6) \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad y \\equiv x \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:6)\n$$\n\nfor all $x,y \\in \\mathbb{Z}.$\n\n- Relation $T$ is symmetric. Indeed, if $(X,Y) \\in T$ then $(Y,X) \\in T.$ Notice that \n\n$$\nX \\cap Y = \\mathbb{N} \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad Y \\cap X = \\mathbb{N}\n$$\n\nfor all $X, Y \\in \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$S$ and $T$ only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-3a2bda0c-9e42-53f1-a525-c0492f5a91dc" :question/id #uuid "3a2bda0c-9e42-53f1-a525-c0492f5a91dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-213473" :question/problem "Consider the following relation defined on the set $A={0,2,5}.$ \n\n$$\nR={(0,0),(0,2),(5,0),(5,2)}\n$$\n\nSelect the options that make the following reasoning correct:\n\nSince for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,{{field-1}}\\,⟹\\,{{field-2}}\\,,\n$$\n\n \nthe relation is \n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x).$\n\nOur relation is defined on ${0,2,5}.$ Notice that:\n\n- $(0,2)\\in R$ and $(2,0)\\notin R$\n\n- $(5,0)\\in R$ and $(0,5)\\notin R$\n\n- $(5,2)\\in R$ and $(2,5)\\notin R$\n\nThere are no other pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ in the relation. So, for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the relation is  $\\text{antisymmetric}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8a604a5c-1a1e-5a3d-addc-b224a59bd107" "answer-field-e59ed41d-6f63-5211-9981-7094e0a62bd3" "answer-field-07a8c960-fbd6-58c0-9b4d-29380f121ae1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8a604a5c-1a1e-5a3d-addc-b224a59bd107" :answer-field/id #uuid "8a604a5c-1a1e-5a3d-