89f1e328-7766-560f-a59a-768449d3254c" "answer-e0ccd994-8aee-591a-b3bc-c42c2871801a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-475315bc-69fe-5c80-a313-bd86899565da" :answer/id #uuid "475315bc-69fe-5c80-a313-bd86899565da" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-89f1e328-7766-560f-a59a-768449d3254c" :answer/id #uuid "89f1e328-7766-560f-a59a-768449d3254c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0ccd994-8aee-591a-b3bc-c42c2871801a" :answer/id #uuid "e0ccd994-8aee-591a-b3bc-c42c2871801a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{?}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-92e553e5-9efc-54d5-9f9a-59a998ba0a7b" :question/id #uuid "92e553e5-9efc-54d5-9f9a-59a998ba0a7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-213492" :question/problem "![](images/88/88e4ad0704f54f33241180593dc1ce4fe384710d5a680717f7c0449d5f85ec2c.png)\n\nWhich of the plots above represents a relation that is neither symmetric nor antisymmetric defined on the set $A={a,b,c}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn terms of a plot, if a point $(x,y)$ belongs to the plot, then $(y,x),$ the point symmetric to $(x,y)$ over the diagonal line $y=x,$ should also be on the plot.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nSo, only one of the points, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be on the plot of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows points on the diagonal line $y=x.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's draw the diagonal line $y=x$ and examine our plots.\n\n![](images/cd/cd4c4ac0b0b4c00e8cb29e8c95738d5c9c4404113c5370791e2277ca66cdbda2.png)\n\n \n\n- Plot I represents an antisymmetric relation since the plot contains at most one point from each pair of the points symmetric over the line $y=x.$\n\n- Plot II represents neither a symmetric nor an antisymmetric relation. It's not symmetric since, for example, the pair $(a,b)$ belongs to the plot while the point $(b,a)$ does not. And it's not antisymmetric since it contains the pairs $(a,c)$ and $(c,a).$\n\n- Plot III represents a symmetric relation since the plot is symmetric over the line $y=x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-51bc889b-d302-50fa-aa28-ad159b53a97d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-51bc889b-d302-50fa-aa28-ad159b53a97d" :answer-field/id #uuid "51bc889b-d302-50fa-aa28-ad159b53a97d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-353df1a9-635f-5637-a998-3ec12de5fb5d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-20bd4423-75c9-5c3e-b7e3-27c624187a6e" "answer-03d90713-bab4-5982-9789-c8022a2a41da" "answer-563b2cb6-9661-5e33-b3af-530622715f23" "answer-353df1a9-635f-5637-a998-3ec12de5fb5d" "answer-96510da6-be83-5df5-9b88-5179df3edb1f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-20bd4423-75c9-5c3e-b7e3-27c624187a6e" :answer/id #uuid "20bd4423-75c9-5c3e-b7e3-27c624187a6e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-03d90713-bab4-5982-9789-c8022a2a41da" :answer/id #uuid "03d90713-bab4-5982-9789-c8022a2a41da" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-563b2cb6-9661-5e33-b3af-530622715f23" :answer/id #uuid "563b2cb6-9661-5e33-b3af-530622715f23" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-353df1a9-635f-5637-a998-3ec12de5fb5d" :answer/id #uuid "353df1a9-635f-5637-a998-3ec12de5fb5d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-96510da6-be83-5df5-9b88-5179df3edb1f" :answer/id #uuid "96510da6-be83-5df5-9b88-5179df3edb1f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204256 :question/problem "![](images/fd/fdbcfe164fcca0e005255de10f10c10bedc156f3a582eeb5800322d4c4ebe0ab.png)\n\nWhich of the plots above represent symmetric relations defined on the set $A={0,2,4}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn terms of a plot, if a point $(x,y)$ belongs to the plot, then $(y,x),$ the point symmetric to $(x,y)$ over the diagonal line $y=x,$ should also be on the plot.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nSo, only one of the points, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be on the plot of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows points on the diagonal line $y=x.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's draw the diagonal line $y=x$ and examine our plots.\n\n![](images/9c/9c9c16300cfb95a61a261e39db865d5c6d056794bd7aaa422567bde10dd4abf5.png)\n\n \n\n- Plot I represents a symmetric relation since the plot is symmetric over the line $y=x.$\n\n- Plot II represents an antisymmetric relation since the plot contains at most one point from each pair of the points symmetric over the line $y=x.$\n\n- Plot III represents neither a symmetric nor an antisymmetric relation. It's not symmetric since, for example, the pair $(4,2)$ belongs to the plot while the point $(2,4)$ does not. And it's not antisymmetric since it contains the pairs $(0,4)$ and $(4,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c99522de-cf4d-5f54-a8c5-8dd127c69656"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c99522de-cf4d-5f54-a8c5-8dd127c69656" :answer-field/id #uuid "c99522de-cf4d-5f54-a8c5-8dd127c69656" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2a78b387-8979-566f-83e2-812eb5fdf2ad" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c70c4806-8925-5b32-88f9-652a7f9a3a9f" "answer-202044b9-b12b-566f-9106-e35394fe4818" "answer-2a78b387-8979-566f-83e2-812eb5fdf2ad" "answer-aeff68f4-b03f-5d82-baed-3043b8eda361" "answer-5b0851ee-3280-534b-892e-859a3e15955f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c70c4806-8925-5b32-88f9-652a7f9a3a9f" :answer/id #uuid "c70c4806-8925-5b32-88f9-652a7f9a3a9f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-202044b9-b12b-566f-9106-e35394fe4818" :answer/id #uuid "202044b9-b12b-566f-9106-e35394fe4818" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2a78b387-8979-566f-83e2-812eb5fdf2ad" :answer/id #uuid "2a78b387-8979-566f-83e2-812eb5fdf2ad" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aeff68f4-b03f-5d82-baed-3043b8eda361" :answer/id #uuid "aeff68f4-b03f-5d82-baed-3043b8eda361" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5b0851ee-3280-534b-892e-859a3e15955f" :answer/id #uuid "5b0851ee-3280-534b-892e-859a3e15955f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-84454995-d49e-5edd-80f6-dfbaab2558d5" :question/id #uuid "84454995-d49e-5edd-80f6-dfbaab2558d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-213318" :question/problem "![](images/a8/a8ebf81e13d905d36997abf2f16c3732714b4feb56334f5e0c56443aa07732c6.png)\n\nWhich of the above digraphs represents an antisymmetric relation defined on the set $A={1,2,3}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn terms of a digraph, if we have an arrow $x\\to y,$ then the arrow $y\\to x$ must be in the digraph too.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nSo, only one of the arrows, either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ can be in the digraph of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows loops.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our digraphs.\n\n- Digraph I represents an antisymmetric relation since for all possible $x$ and $y$ with $x\\ne y,$ *at most one* of the arrows (either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ but not both) belongs to the digraph.\n\n- Digraph II represents neither a symmetric nor an antisymmetric relation. It's not symmetric since the arrow $3\\to 2$ belongs to the digraph while the arrow $2\\to 3$ does not. It is not antisymmetric since, for example, arrows $1\\to 2$ and $2\\to 1$ belong to the digraph.\n\n- Digraph III represents a symmetric relation since for every arrow $x\\to y$ belonging to the graph, the corresponding inverse arrow $y\\to x$ also belongs to the graph.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5a672eb5-09d4-52a3-990d-6a002fb2c6dc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5a672eb5-09d4-52a3-990d-6a002fb2c6dc" :answer-field/id #uuid "5a672eb5-09d4-52a3-990d-6a002fb2c6dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1dc38fe7-4cc0-5b89-838a-99b8f54f414a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-32e8c8fd-be74-587d-a3b4-8214f956aaa8" "answer-1dc38fe7-4cc0-5b89-838a-99b8f54f414a" "answer-12c5373d-0629-56d7-901e-7af879dd9ae2" "answer-ae7470ff-d87b-54d1-8fb8-68a8dbdf78fc" "answer-8281f135-368b-5bd3-8ea